                            <a name="comment2216"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Making It Up?
</h4>

<p>
"Kurtz Today"  <br />
<br />
Good article full of facts.  As I read it, a flurry of points and refutations ran through my mind; however, Mark Feldstein summarizes my position more pointedly:<br />
<br />
"There is a cloud over the New Republic, but there's one hanging over the Army, as well. Each investigated this and cleared themselves, but they both have vested interests." <br />
<br />
If the Weekly Standard and The New Republic have honestly attempted to vet their stories, then I don&#8217;t have a problem with either.  It does not take a leap of faith to understand how they could have come to opposite conclusions.  We come back then to the origins of truth.  All are telling the truth, it&#8217;s just that they are different truths&#8212;unnamed sources vs. unnamed sources.<br />
<br />
Ben, I&#8217;m thoroughly enjoying your Blog.  Keep it going!<br />
<br />

</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:alex_e_peraza@yahoo.com">Alex Peraza</a>
	

 at August 08,2007 14:12
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment2217"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Making It Up?
</h4>

<p>
Huh? <br />
<br />
Beauchamp either did or didn't make shit up? <br />
<br />
Right?<br />
<br />
How does "an affidavit acknowledging that his three articles were filled with exaggerations and falsehoods"  exist in the same universe, at the same time, as "a statement that did not contradict his writings for the New Republic?"<br />
<br />
What one of these people is saying is not true, in the less-dynamic, practical sense of the word.<br />
<br />
Reporting and writing is subjective, everyone here knows that. Choose a detail, choose a quote, you shape it in the retelling. But what you write - as long as you're not a liar - is true, for all intents and purposes.<br />
<br />
I mean, you were at the scene, you saw it, you heard the person say it, you wrote it down and got it in the paper. <br />
<br />
But when you make things up, invent people and scenarios, that isn't...uh...true. Right?<br />
<br />
Why are we getting rubbery with the truth? Frey-style?
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		ralrika	

 at August 08,2007 16:14
</div>
